 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Identical Depth Fragments:** Standard peeling outputs only one fragment for identical depths, causing incorrect `insideFlags`. This occurs when common points of two surfaces project exactly to pixel centers or when back-facing surfaces touch front-facing surfaces of the same VOI (common at corners/edges of non-empty-space bounding boxes) [S1].
-*   **Hardware Depth Test Limitations:** Modifying depth in a fragment program may disable hardware depth testing on some GPUs. In such cases, the encoded depth must be written to a color channel and compared using MIN/MAX frame buffer operations [S1].
+*   **Identical Depth Fragments:** Standard peeling outputs only one fragment for identical depths, causing incorrect `insideFlags` [S1]. This occurs when common points of two surfaces project exactly to pixel centers, or when a back-facing surface touches a front-facing surface of the same VOI at corners/edges of non-empty-space bounding boxes [S1].
+*   **Hardware Depth Test Limitations:** Modifying depth in a fragment program may disable hardware depth testing on some GPUs [S1]. In such cases, the encoded depth must be written to a color channel and compared using MIN/MAX frame buffer operations [S1].
 
